1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:09,040
From a simple billy club to an arsenal of weapons designed to sniff out the tiniest clue

2
00:00:09,041 --> 00:00:16,160
or knock down the toughest bad guy. Now, police technology on Modern Marvels.

3
00:00:16,161 --> 00:00:30,161
Clad in full body armor and ballistic helmets, with goggles that can see through darkness and smoke,

4
00:00:35,840 --> 00:00:38,260
and weapons that can stun, shock, and disable,

5
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:41,900
today's SWAT teams are the embodiment of high-tech cops.

6
00:00:41,901 --> 00:00:46,520
and the future is just around the corner.

7
00:00:48,220 --> 00:00:53,020
The Urban Robot is a surveillance device designed to go places no human would dare.

8
00:00:54,300 --> 00:01:01,680
Able to climb stairs, maneuver through tight spaces, open doors, and even blow a hole through a wall.

9
00:01:01,681 --> 00:01:12,680
It has video, audio, and even infrared sensors for letting those on the outside know what's going on inside.

10
00:01:12,681 --> 00:01:21,680
In case of danger, it can hide from sight or escape to safety from a second story window.

11
00:01:21,681 --> 00:01:30,120
The urban robot may or may not be the policeman of the future, but it is certainly a considerable

12
00:01:30,121 --> 00:01:34,840
change from the policeman of the past.

13
00:01:34,841 --> 00:01:39,780
The precursors to modern day police date back at least as far as the Roman Empire's Praetorian

14
00:01:39,781 --> 00:01:40,780
Guard.

15
00:01:40,781 --> 00:01:47,080
Essentially a private army, they wielded their swords to protect the emperor and do his dirty

16
00:01:47,081 --> 00:01:49,000
work.

17
00:01:49,001 --> 00:01:52,580
In the Middle Ages, nobles appointed local constables to uphold the law.

18
00:01:53,260 --> 00:01:56,100
But for the most part, they were unpaid and unarmed.

19
00:01:56,840 --> 00:02:02,000
In 17th century France, Louis XIV established a small central police force

20
00:02:02,001 --> 00:02:08,080
whose main job was to spy on dissidents and, when necessary, suppress them.

21
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:14,760
But the first true police force as we know it wasn't formed until 1829 in London, England.

22
00:02:15,460 --> 00:02:19,780
Their purpose, singular purpose, was to make sure that people behave themselves in public.

23
00:02:21,120 --> 00:02:25,940
And they would deal with crimes as they occurred, but they weren't created to be proactive to deal with crime.

24
00:02:27,140 --> 00:02:33,540
Dubbed Bobbies after their founders, Sir Robert Peel, they were armed with little more than a short wooden billy club.

25
00:02:35,040 --> 00:02:40,060
To this day, the billy club remains the only weapon carried by the majority of London police.

26
00:02:40,061 --> 00:02:46,700
In the United States, police trace their beginnings to night patrols formed in the mid-1600s,

27
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:48,720
like New York's rattle watch.

28
00:02:49,320 --> 00:02:53,240
They were called the rattle watch because the officers that were assigned to this watch

29
00:02:53,241 --> 00:02:58,320
carried large wooden rattles that were used to warn the residents of any type of danger.

30
00:02:58,780 --> 00:03:02,680
The rattles made an extremely loud noise, much like a machine gun would sound today.

31
00:03:03,320 --> 00:03:08,240
In 1820, they were replaced by a fire brigade that became known as the Leatherheads.

32
00:03:08,241 --> 00:03:12,420
The reason why they were called Leatherheads is because they wore a large leather helmet,

33
00:03:12,960 --> 00:03:18,240
which had a large piece of leather draped down the back, much like a firefighter's helmet would be today.

34
00:03:19,220 --> 00:03:22,880
In addition to fighting fires, they were also expected to fight crime.

35
00:03:23,600 --> 00:03:28,200
But New York's booming and unruly population soon proved to be more than they could handle.

36
00:03:29,620 --> 00:03:34,220
In 1845, New York City established its first official police department.

37
00:03:34,720 --> 00:03:37,680
Their copper badges led to the nickname Coppers.

38
00:03:38,240 --> 00:03:39,740
later shortened to cops.

39
00:03:40,040 --> 00:03:44,380
They carried devices that were the predecessor to handcuffs called nippers

40
00:03:44,381 --> 00:03:46,580
that would be twisted around a person's wrists

41
00:03:46,581 --> 00:03:49,100
and held by the officer to control their hands.

42
00:03:50,040 --> 00:03:53,420
Like their British counterparts, they were armed with only a nightstick.

43
00:03:54,040 --> 00:03:57,720
It was thought that an officer who needed a firearm could not be a good officer.

44
00:03:57,920 --> 00:03:59,440
I mean, if you had to resort to shooting someone,

45
00:03:59,540 --> 00:04:00,960
obviously you're doing something wrong.

46
00:04:01,580 --> 00:04:05,880
But that opinion changed when a series of draft riots in the 1870s

47
00:04:05,881 --> 00:04:10,700
made police and the public realize how quickly situations could get out of control.

48
00:04:11,540 --> 00:04:14,460
It made natural sense that officers should be prepared to protect themselves.

49
00:04:14,800 --> 00:04:18,340
If you meet a crowd of ruffians and you hit the first couple with a nightstick,

50
00:04:18,440 --> 00:04:21,240
that leaves the rest of the crowd to beat up on you.

51
00:04:22,160 --> 00:04:26,480
In the 1870s, officers were given permission to carry their own guns,

52
00:04:26,940 --> 00:04:30,080
hidden beneath long frock coats so as not to alarm the public.

53
00:04:30,081 --> 00:04:38,680
It was not until 1887 that the NYPD issued its first official firearm, a Colt .32 revolver.

54
00:04:39,880 --> 00:04:44,900
The daily work of the vast majority of these turn-of-the-century policemen was to walk the beat.

55
00:04:45,840 --> 00:04:48,500
Their beat was a geographical area that they were assigned to.

56
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:54,900
Other than arresting violators that they encountered and responding to calls for help, for assistance from officers,

57
00:04:55,400 --> 00:04:56,940
they were there as problem solvers as well.

58
00:04:56,941 --> 00:04:57,998
The officer was your friend.

59
00:04:57,999 --> 00:05:01,060
If you had a problem, you would go to the officer because, hey, he had a uniform.

60
00:05:01,560 --> 00:05:02,636
That's his job is to help you.

61
00:05:02,637 --> 00:05:05,220
If there's a crime being committed, you would go for the police officer.

62
00:05:05,280 --> 00:05:07,446
If you had a question, if you had a problem or Mrs.

63
00:05:07,447 --> 00:05:09,740
Smith went into labor, you'd go find a police officer.

64
00:05:09,840 --> 00:05:10,540
They were there to help you.

65
00:05:10,800 --> 00:05:15,060
He had no means of communications back to headquarters until later in the century.

66
00:05:15,680 --> 00:05:23,960
And so his way of communicating, if he needed help, was to wrap his baton hard on the concrete and hope that someone would hear it.

67
00:05:23,961 --> 00:05:30,360
But in the late 1800s, the newly invented telephone was put to one of its first practical uses,

68
00:05:30,960 --> 00:05:33,940
with the introduction of the call box, or street telephone.

69
00:05:34,680 --> 00:05:38,500
This gave beat cops their first real link back to their precinct house.

70
00:05:39,280 --> 00:05:41,520
That was a dramatic improvement over beating on the concrete.

71
00:05:41,980 --> 00:05:43,720
You could go to the call box, pick it up.

72
00:05:43,800 --> 00:05:45,720
There was a direct line that went straight to the police.

73
00:05:46,380 --> 00:05:49,280
Call boxes really sort of revolutionized beat patrol work.

74
00:05:49,840 --> 00:05:51,420
You could easily call back to dispatch.

75
00:05:51,421 --> 00:05:56,660
but you could easily get help if you go to the call box, open it up, and you could get to someone in a hurry.

76
00:05:57,800 --> 00:06:01,940
In many cities, call boxes remained in use until the 1960s.

77
00:06:02,120 --> 00:06:07,100
They were operated with a key, which I was assigned, which had my shield number stamped on it.

78
00:06:07,640 --> 00:06:14,940
And at predetermined times, I was expected to open that box with my key,

79
00:06:15,260 --> 00:06:19,840
pick the receiver off, wait for somebody to pick up the other end and tell them that, you know,

80
00:06:19,841 --> 00:06:24,860
Patrolman Flynn, you know, beat number 14, 1745 badge number, on post.

81
00:06:26,400 --> 00:06:30,080
The first vehicles used by police were dubbed paddy wagons,

82
00:06:30,081 --> 00:06:33,380
most likely a slur against their frequent Irish passengers.

83
00:06:34,340 --> 00:06:38,420
Used for transporting prisoners, they were originally open supply wagons,

84
00:06:38,660 --> 00:06:40,460
left over from the Spanish-American War.

85
00:06:41,360 --> 00:06:48,080
But most soon added covers, reportedly to protect passengers from excessive publicity and humiliation.

86
00:06:48,880 --> 00:06:52,340
To combat the problem of high-speed carriages and runaway steeds,

87
00:06:52,860 --> 00:06:55,260
some cities began putting officers on horseback.

88
00:06:56,400 --> 00:07:00,380
In 1895, New York announced the formation of a Scorcher Squad.

89
00:07:01,080 --> 00:07:03,380
There were 25 men on high-speed bicycles.

90
00:07:04,180 --> 00:07:06,280
The reason why they obtained the name of the Scorcher Squad

91
00:07:06,281 --> 00:07:09,220
was because from their smoking wheels of going so quickly.

92
00:07:09,980 --> 00:07:14,800
They were pretty much established to combat the rising traffic condition

93
00:07:14,801 --> 00:07:17,840
with the advent of automobiles into New York City.

94
00:07:18,080 --> 00:07:24,080
To keep pace with faster cars, bikes were soon replaced by motorcycles.

95
00:07:24,081 --> 00:07:28,080
And it wasn't long before police got automobiles of their own.

96
00:07:28,081 --> 00:07:32,080
These first police cars were notable for their political sensitivity.

97
00:07:32,081 --> 00:07:36,080
Sirens weren't allowed because they scared people.

98
00:07:36,081 --> 00:07:40,080
Some departments required officers to ride an open-top convertibles.

99
00:07:40,081 --> 00:07:44,080
In the early part of the use of patrol guards by the New York State Police,

100
00:07:44,081 --> 00:07:48,480
police they bought convurals because it was felt that if you drive around in a car that has

101
00:07:48,481 --> 00:07:54,080
a ceiling on it people are going to lose touch with you as a state trooper now western york as

102
00:07:54,081 --> 00:07:59,360
i said is very cold imagine being a police officer in 1930s driving around with the flaps up on their

103
00:07:59,361 --> 00:08:06,320
coats and big bulky hats on and and goggles virtually driving it's very very amusing stuff

104
00:08:08,160 --> 00:08:12,240
by the 1930s police cars began to be equipped with radios

105
00:08:12,241 --> 00:08:14,380
The first car radios were one-way radios.

106
00:08:14,980 --> 00:08:18,280
One-way radio, the dispatcher would contact the patrol car,

107
00:08:18,480 --> 00:08:20,480
request them to contact the precinct station house.

108
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:22,960
They would then have to go to a call box

109
00:08:22,961 --> 00:08:25,020
and confirm that they received the message

110
00:08:25,021 --> 00:08:27,640
because they weren't able to communicate back through that same radio.

111
00:08:28,460 --> 00:08:31,600
As cars became more and more ubiquitous in police departments,

112
00:08:32,120 --> 00:08:33,920
you could dispatch police from a central point.

113
00:08:33,921 --> 00:08:35,740
This is efficient.

114
00:08:36,260 --> 00:08:39,280
It means you can send a police officer wherever they need it,

115
00:08:39,281 --> 00:08:45,080
You don't have to have a police officer who is basically isolated, trapped to a beat to walk only that beat.

116
00:08:45,760 --> 00:08:47,940
That sounds very good and very efficient.

117
00:08:48,260 --> 00:08:54,500
The problem is that it meant now that police officers were in cars responding to calls for service.

118
00:08:55,180 --> 00:08:58,840
What they weren't doing now was walking the beat, getting to know the neighborhood.

119
00:08:59,340 --> 00:09:05,840
It went from being Officer Smith, who is our police officer, to being a nameless, faceless person in a patrol car,

120
00:09:05,841 --> 00:09:09,800
usually two at this time period, driving around and stopping only when they saw

121
00:09:09,801 --> 00:09:14,040
indications of criminal behavior. By the end of the Second World War, police

122
00:09:14,041 --> 00:09:18,560
technology had come a long way, but police were soon to find out how far

123
00:09:18,561 --> 00:09:27,600
they still had to go. We now return to police technology on Modern Marvels.

124
00:09:28,320 --> 00:09:34,760
As criminals got better armed, so did the police. As early as the 1930s, rifles and

125
00:09:34,761 --> 00:09:38,884
shotguns began to be a standard part of the police arsenal.

126
00:09:38,885 --> 00:09:41,540
In the 1960s, departments slowly began

127
00:09:41,541 --> 00:09:46,640
replacing the six-shot revolver with semi-automatic pistols that could hold up to 16 rounds.

128
00:09:48,480 --> 00:09:55,100
But despite these arsenals, in the early 1960s, urban unrest and crime started to increase rapidly.

129
00:09:56,480 --> 00:09:59,956
Crime had taken a dramatic upturn in the mid-60s.

130
00:09:59,957 --> 00:10:03,220
In fact, if you go back and look at statistics

131
00:10:03,221 --> 00:10:05,600
in the Census Bureau and other places,

132
00:10:05,601 --> 00:10:10,360
you see this remarkable uptick in crime beginning about 1965.

133
00:10:11,320 --> 00:10:15,500
In 1965, President Lyndon Johnson convened a presidential commission

134
00:10:15,501 --> 00:10:17,440
on law enforcement and administration,

135
00:10:18,200 --> 00:10:20,060
better known as the Crime Commission.

136
00:10:20,940 --> 00:10:24,320
That commission looked at how police worked,

137
00:10:24,860 --> 00:10:27,760
and it had a piece that looked at science and technology as well.

138
00:10:27,761 --> 00:10:33,260
Its role was to say, how can we as a nation begin to address this problem?

139
00:10:33,261 --> 00:10:35,460
And one of their answers was technology.

140
00:10:35,461 --> 00:10:37,360
We need to have more technology.

141
00:10:37,361 --> 00:10:38,760
We need to make better use of technology.

142
00:10:38,761 --> 00:10:43,260
We need to make, we need to pass that technology on to the officers that are in the field.

143
00:10:43,261 --> 00:10:52,260
Policing in 1960 was essentially what it was, technology speaking, in the early 1900s.

144
00:10:52,261 --> 00:10:56,960
If you carried any technology at all, it was a handheld radio, and you may have arrived in a car instead of a horse.

145
00:10:56,961 --> 00:10:58,960
Those were the key differences.

146
00:10:58,961 --> 00:11:01,960
And at the time the Crime Commission report came forward,

147
00:11:01,961 --> 00:11:05,960
they noted all these dramatic changes in technology in the general society,

148
00:11:05,961 --> 00:11:08,960
but very few such changes in law enforcement.

149
00:11:08,961 --> 00:11:14,960
One of the Commission's recommendations was to create a single nationwide emergency telephone number.

150
00:11:14,961 --> 00:11:17,960
Some of the audience will remember when they were younger,

151
00:11:17,961 --> 00:11:22,960
before the days of 9-1-1, when you had the number of the police department taped next to the telephone.

152
00:11:22,961 --> 00:11:25,960
Wherever you happened to be, that number was different.

153
00:11:25,961 --> 00:11:30,200
9-1-1, the concept of having a single emergency number, actually came from Great Britain.

154
00:11:30,700 --> 00:11:32,660
And I think the numbers they use is 9-9-9.

155
00:11:33,520 --> 00:11:39,500
AT&T, the giant monopoly that it was in the 60s and 70s, decided to use 9-1-1 as the number.

156
00:11:39,620 --> 00:11:41,300
They thought it was very easy for people to remember.

157
00:11:41,860 --> 00:11:47,200
It wasn't a single set of digits, so you can't have children inadvertently click 1-1-1 or 9-9-9.

158
00:11:48,420 --> 00:11:53,840
In early 1968, an Alabama phone company office manager named Bill Fry

159
00:11:53,841 --> 00:11:57,380
rerouted local lines to enable 911 dialing.

160
00:11:58,380 --> 00:12:04,100
On February 16, 1968, the first 911 call in the U.S. was made

161
00:12:04,101 --> 00:12:06,540
in the small town of Haleyville, Alabama.

162
00:12:07,540 --> 00:12:12,460
That same year, New York became the first major city to install a 911 system.

163
00:12:13,440 --> 00:12:14,240
Ocean, Sam.

164
00:12:14,241 --> 00:12:19,160
Over the next three decades, 911 spread to over 85% of the country.

165
00:12:20,240 --> 00:12:23,480
Most current systems use enhanced 911 technology,

166
00:12:23,840 --> 00:12:26,760
which displays the caller's name and address automatically.

167
00:12:27,380 --> 00:12:28,080
Okay.

168
00:12:28,540 --> 00:12:33,400
In this country alone, the system now fields over 100 million calls every year.

169
00:12:33,401 --> 00:12:34,160
That's Plaza One.

170
00:12:34,860 --> 00:12:38,300
Another result of the crime commission was a flood of federal grants

171
00:12:38,301 --> 00:12:40,520
to equip police departments with computers.

172
00:12:41,780 --> 00:12:43,440
Computerized equipment was very popular.

173
00:12:43,500 --> 00:12:44,200
It was a big thing.

174
00:12:44,320 --> 00:12:46,820
Let's give police departments money to buy their own computers.

175
00:12:47,240 --> 00:12:49,920
It wound up being a terrible fiasco.

176
00:12:49,921 --> 00:12:54,800
There were instances of computers, whole mainframes or many computers,

177
00:12:54,801 --> 00:12:58,120
that were purchased and installed in apartments and never turned on

178
00:12:58,121 --> 00:13:00,720
because they didn't have people to operate them.

179
00:13:00,721 --> 00:13:02,720
You have a bunch of officers who are like, well, it's a computer.

180
00:13:02,721 --> 00:13:04,240
What do I know about ones and zeros?

181
00:13:04,241 --> 00:13:08,720
Many computers wound up sitting in rooms, become very expensive filing cabinets.

182
00:13:08,721 --> 00:13:11,120
So it was just terrible.

183
00:13:11,121 --> 00:13:15,120
I think that technology was still in its infancy.

184
00:13:15,121 --> 00:13:18,800
And any technology that's going to have a police application

185
00:13:18,801 --> 00:13:20,520
has to be robust.

186
00:13:23,020 --> 00:13:25,880
Research and development efforts by the Justice Department

187
00:13:25,881 --> 00:13:28,100
quickly dwindled down to a single man

188
00:13:28,101 --> 00:13:30,640
who was destined to become one of the great pioneers

189
00:13:30,641 --> 00:13:34,020
of police technology, chemist Lester Shubin.

190
00:13:34,720 --> 00:13:37,100
I got into it partly because

191
00:13:37,101 --> 00:13:40,800
maybe it's just my mind.

192
00:13:42,400 --> 00:13:44,960
Nobody else seemed to be picking some of these things up.

193
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:48,400
One of Shubin's first ideas was inspired

194
00:13:48,401 --> 00:13:49,427
by his pet dog.

195
00:13:49,428 --> 00:13:55,040
I had a hunting dog that had been trained and I used to throw a stick out into the

196
00:13:55,041 --> 00:13:59,720
woods and even if I was wearing gloves he'd bring the same stick back and I thought if a dog could

197
00:13:59,721 --> 00:14:04,700
do that you know a thousand other sticks out there just like it if he could bring back the

198
00:14:04,701 --> 00:14:08,204
same stick all the time then they ought to be able to do something else.

199
00:14:08,205 --> 00:14:09,080
Well I went to the

200
00:14:09,081 --> 00:14:16,580
army at Aberdeen and we worked out a training system and we found remarkably they could keep

201
00:14:16,581 --> 00:14:24,980
finding these explosives and almost 100 success despite the successful results few took shubin's

202
00:14:24,981 --> 00:14:30,900
dogs seriously i was the butt of some jokes what are you going to do when the contract ends where

203
00:14:30,901 --> 00:14:36,980
you're going to put the dogs and i said well i think we're going to kennel them down here in

204
00:14:36,981 --> 00:14:45,940
personnel and so they decided to give the dogs to new york city in march 1972 shortly after a

205
00:14:45,941 --> 00:14:49,040
a TWA jet took off from Kennedy Airport.

206
00:14:49,041 --> 00:14:51,340
Authorities received a phone call informing them

207
00:14:51,341 --> 00:14:53,760
that a bomb was on board.

208
00:14:53,761 --> 00:14:58,320
The caller demanded $2 million to reveal its location.

209
00:14:58,321 --> 00:15:00,820
The jet immediately returned to Kennedy Airport,

210
00:15:00,821 --> 00:15:02,660
where Brandy, one of Shubin's dogs,

211
00:15:02,661 --> 00:15:06,040
found the bomb in just 12 minutes.

212
00:15:06,041 --> 00:15:08,500
The positive publicity allowed Shubin the freedom

213
00:15:08,501 --> 00:15:10,240
to explore other ideas.

214
00:15:10,241 --> 00:15:13,340
Well, Lester early on saw an advertisement in a magazine.

215
00:15:13,341 --> 00:15:15,700
And in this ad, it had somebody hanging

216
00:15:15,701 --> 00:15:18,500
from a single strand of cord, and it said,

217
00:15:18,900 --> 00:15:20,960
Kevlar is stronger than steel.

218
00:15:21,340 --> 00:15:25,380
Well, Lester was intrigued by this idea.

219
00:15:26,400 --> 00:15:28,580
Kevlar was being developed by DuPont

220
00:15:28,581 --> 00:15:30,800
for use in steel-belted radial tires.

221
00:15:31,500 --> 00:15:34,920
I wondered at that time if you had something like this,

222
00:15:34,960 --> 00:15:35,820
if it would stop a bullet.

223
00:15:36,780 --> 00:15:40,680
We got some material, folded it up, took it out, shot it,

224
00:15:40,681 --> 00:15:42,440
and the bullets bounced off.

225
00:15:42,441 --> 00:15:48,320
Bulletproof garments had been around since the 1930s.

226
00:15:51,540 --> 00:15:56,940
But they were made of metal plates or heavy nylon and were impractical for everyday use.

227
00:16:01,740 --> 00:16:06,600
Though Kevlar vests were lighter and thinner, at first few police wanted to wear them.

228
00:16:06,601 --> 00:16:13,680
Their response was, it's bulky, it's hot, it's expensive, it's impractical, and besides, it's not macho, I'm not going to wear this stuff.

229
00:16:14,240 --> 00:16:15,760
And so they didn't want anything to do with it.

230
00:16:16,380 --> 00:16:25,040
So Lester's approach then was to find enough funding to manufacture 5,000 units of body armor.

231
00:16:25,440 --> 00:16:27,880
Put them on police, we're providing it free as a test.

232
00:16:28,660 --> 00:16:34,660
In late 1975, Shubin's vests were given away to police departments in 15 cities.

233
00:16:34,661 --> 00:16:42,040
Two days before Christmas, a Seattle patrolman named Ray Johnson was shot twice at near point-blank range.

234
00:16:43,400 --> 00:16:46,240
Fortunately, he was wearing one of Shubin's vests.

235
00:16:46,840 --> 00:16:51,400
I got a call that it happened. They kept the officer in the hospital overnight.

236
00:16:52,300 --> 00:16:55,420
He was very unhappy because he missed Christmas at home.

237
00:16:56,260 --> 00:16:59,600
But he had two deep bruises on his chest, but he survived the shot.

238
00:17:00,020 --> 00:17:04,380
That incident convinced police across the country of the value of body armor.

239
00:17:04,660 --> 00:17:11,720
By the end of the century, Shubin's vests had been credited for saving the lives of well over 2,500 officers.

240
00:17:12,740 --> 00:17:19,160
Today, they are lighter, more effective, and appropriately also available for dogs, all thanks to Lester Shubin.

241
00:17:19,800 --> 00:17:25,020
The legacy he left, that is a way of developing a technology, proving that it actually works,

242
00:17:25,480 --> 00:17:31,600
ensuring its quality and getting in the field, is in fact a model that, somewhat enhanced, we still use.

243
00:17:31,601 --> 00:17:37,540
So he really established the foundation of how to develop a technology for the law enforcement community.

244
00:17:38,320 --> 00:17:42,520
Coming up, a Supreme Court decision that led to a revolution in detective work.

245
00:17:43,640 --> 00:17:47,660
The second place in the U.S. to have 911 was Nome, Alaska.

246
00:17:48,380 --> 00:17:51,240
Police technology will return on Modern Marvelous.

247
00:17:52,940 --> 00:17:57,100
We now return to police technology on Modern Marvelous.

248
00:17:57,101 --> 00:18:07,740
In 1963, a 23-year-old truck driver named Ernesto Miranda was arrested in Arizona on charges of kidnapping and rape.

249
00:18:08,960 --> 00:18:12,820
After two hours of questioning, he confessed to both crimes.

250
00:18:13,820 --> 00:18:15,560
Miranda's confession was nothing unusual.

251
00:18:16,340 --> 00:18:21,320
It had become an unwritten rule of police work that the best way to get a conviction was to get a confession.

252
00:18:22,260 --> 00:18:26,700
In 1967, the Supreme Court said that had to change.

253
00:18:27,100 --> 00:18:35,220
The Supreme Court in the Miranda case said officers are being taught how to manipulate people into saying things against their interests.

254
00:18:35,760 --> 00:18:38,740
Not unlike a good salesman or a good attorney.

255
00:18:39,240 --> 00:18:41,140
And what the Supreme Court said is, no, wait a minute.

256
00:18:41,640 --> 00:18:49,710
You know, there's certain aspects that are probably okay, but everybody in this game has to at least start on a reasonably level playing field, and that's where this

257
00:18:49,711 --> 00:18:51,080
warning is going to come in.

258
00:18:51,220 --> 00:18:52,280
You have the right to remain silent.

259
00:18:52,700 --> 00:18:54,360
You have the right to an attorney, et cetera, et cetera.

260
00:18:54,361 --> 00:18:56,700
And officers were told, you have to say these things.

261
00:18:56,701 --> 00:18:59,640
And if you don't, the bad guy may be let go.

262
00:19:00,640 --> 00:19:02,620
Huge change in how police work took place.

263
00:19:03,120 --> 00:19:06,000
And it actually helped in some respects to professionalize police work

264
00:19:06,001 --> 00:19:10,500
because officers had to learn how to use the law properly.

265
00:19:10,620 --> 00:19:11,880
They had to collect good evidence.

266
00:19:13,420 --> 00:19:17,460
Miranda created a demand for better evidence-gathering techniques and technologies.

267
00:19:18,280 --> 00:19:23,923
In the 1970s alone, fostered by federal grants, the number of crime labs in the U.S.

268
00:19:23,924 --> 00:19:24,624
doubled.

269
00:19:24,625 --> 00:19:31,400
Evidence gathering technology dates back to the late 1800s, when the Berta-Lon system was

270
00:19:31,401 --> 00:19:35,700
introduced as a scientific means of recording and determining a person's true identity.

271
00:19:36,640 --> 00:19:40,780
That system relied on precise measurements of a suspect's bone structure.

272
00:19:41,520 --> 00:19:46,860
It was really based primarily on the thought that people's bones did not continue to grow

273
00:19:46,861 --> 00:19:49,420
after the age of 20, which we know it from.

274
00:19:49,421 --> 00:19:56,840
By the turn of the century, it had been abandoned in favor of a new method developed by the British, fingerprinting.

275
00:19:57,920 --> 00:19:59,746
The FBI lab in Washington, D.C.

276
00:19:59,747 --> 00:20:03,220
amassed a huge collection of prints of convicted criminals.

277
00:20:06,060 --> 00:20:12,780
But when it came to determining who an unidentified fingerprint belonged to, the technology had its drawbacks.

278
00:20:12,781 --> 00:20:22,900
I think a generation of people grew up thinking that if we got a fingerprint at a crime scene,

279
00:20:23,080 --> 00:20:26,060
we could find out who committed the crime.

280
00:20:26,800 --> 00:20:28,460
I thought that's the way it worked.

281
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:32,880
I went to the police academy, and I was astounded to find out that, you know,

282
00:20:33,040 --> 00:20:36,260
fingerprints were virtually useless unless we had an identified suspect.

283
00:20:36,800 --> 00:20:41,280
If you have fingerprints from a suspect and you have 60,000 fingerprint records,

284
00:20:41,281 --> 00:20:47,880
A person, a single person, has to go through manually through every single print on file that they come across with.

285
00:20:48,620 --> 00:20:54,520
In the 1970s, the FBI introduced AFIS, the Automated Fingerprint Identification System.

286
00:20:55,100 --> 00:20:58,420
All of a sudden, fingerprinting took on a much bigger turn, the FIS,

287
00:20:59,060 --> 00:21:03,680
which would allow fingerprints to be taken from a suspect and then fed into a system

288
00:21:03,681 --> 00:21:07,540
which would use a computer database and a computer to analyze the submissions

289
00:21:07,541 --> 00:21:11,120
and compare them very quickly to files that were on record.

290
00:21:11,280 --> 00:21:22,760
In bottom line, it means that criminals that heretofore would have walked away and potentially fled a jurisdiction will now be arrested.

291
00:21:22,761 --> 00:21:29,780
In L.A., they found a murderer in half an hour going through X amount of records.

292
00:21:29,880 --> 00:21:33,280
It would have taken a technician 67 years to do it by hand.

293
00:21:34,600 --> 00:21:38,140
Gathering fingerprints at a crime scene was also a tedious process.

294
00:21:38,141 --> 00:21:41,520
that entailed brushing surfaces with a graphite powder.

295
00:21:42,300 --> 00:21:45,640
The problem with that kind of approach is that it's a very faint print.

296
00:21:45,740 --> 00:21:46,620
It may not pick it up.

297
00:21:46,621 --> 00:21:50,540
If you brush the material on too aggressively,

298
00:21:50,700 --> 00:21:52,740
it may brush the print away, and so you may lose it.

299
00:21:53,880 --> 00:21:57,900
In the 1970s, the Japanese developed a way of recovering prints

300
00:21:57,901 --> 00:22:02,040
using cyanoacrylate acid, a chemical found in superglue.

301
00:22:03,160 --> 00:22:07,160
Objects were placed in a sealed chamber that was then filled with a superglue fume

302
00:22:07,161 --> 00:22:09,000
that made the prints visible.

303
00:22:09,940 --> 00:22:12,580
More recently, vapor wands have been developed

304
00:22:12,581 --> 00:22:15,740
that allow investigators to use this technique in the field.

305
00:22:16,100 --> 00:22:17,760
One version is a little bitty wand,

306
00:22:18,420 --> 00:22:21,640
almost like a small handheld propane torch

307
00:22:21,641 --> 00:22:24,040
that's used to fume things locally.

308
00:22:24,200 --> 00:22:25,200
That can do a number of prints,

309
00:22:25,300 --> 00:22:26,800
especially prints in very awkward places

310
00:22:26,801 --> 00:22:28,280
where the powder doesn't work real well,

311
00:22:28,600 --> 00:22:30,940
like underneath a brass rail.

312
00:22:31,740 --> 00:22:34,440
In the late 1980s, another form of evidence

313
00:22:34,441 --> 00:22:36,260
began to be recognized by the courts,

314
00:22:36,261 --> 00:22:36,961
DNA.

315
00:22:36,962 --> 00:22:42,940
DNA is probably one of our most important and most interesting technologies now because

316
00:22:42,941 --> 00:22:45,262
it works so powerfully in both directions.

317
00:22:45,263 --> 00:22:47,640
It helps catch the guilty and it helps make

318
00:22:47,641 --> 00:22:51,230
sure we don't inadvertently convict the innocent.

319
00:22:51,231 --> 00:22:54,380
DNA technologies increasingly are useful in

320
00:22:54,381 --> 00:22:59,780
any part of the human body we get, whether it's hair, fingerprints, blood, saliva, virtually

321
00:22:59,781 --> 00:23:03,740
anything we can get off a body or that's left by a body we can get.

322
00:23:03,741 --> 00:23:05,100
Since bodies, the human

323
00:23:05,101 --> 00:23:09,780
body is shedding thousands of cells constantly, it's very difficult to go anywhere without

324
00:23:09,781 --> 00:23:15,040
leaving some trace behind that might be useful in a forensic setting.

325
00:23:15,620 --> 00:23:19,966
DNA is an important part of forensic technology because it's virtually inescapable.

326
00:23:19,967 --> 00:23:20,667
If you

327
00:23:20,668 --> 00:23:25,160
have crime scene evidence with DNA and you take DNA evidence from a suspect and the two

328
00:23:25,161 --> 00:23:32,020
match, it's one in a million chance that it's not correct, far beyond the possible incorrectness

329
00:23:32,021 --> 00:23:32,721
of fingerprints.

330
00:23:32,722 --> 00:23:39,060
In the near future, it will be possible to gather and analyze DNA samples in the field

331
00:23:39,061 --> 00:23:41,800
using this credit card-sized miniature lab.

332
00:23:43,020 --> 00:23:48,380
A drop of DNA evidence dissolved in a solution is placed on a microchip that is able to read

333
00:23:48,381 --> 00:23:53,360
the DNA fingerprint, which can then be sent to a central database for comparison.

334
00:23:56,240 --> 00:24:01,120
But technology's greatest contribution to evidence collection has to do with new ways

335
00:24:01,121 --> 00:24:03,720
of storing it and then finding it again.

336
00:24:04,480 --> 00:24:10,720
When I first started out, most of our information was handwritten on a paper report and stored

337
00:24:10,721 --> 00:24:11,780
in filing cabinets.

338
00:24:12,560 --> 00:24:16,600
Well, if I had a name, if I wanted to see what kind of information I had on a person,

339
00:24:16,601 --> 00:24:22,680
or if I had an address, if I wanted to find out information about any previous police activity

340
00:24:22,681 --> 00:24:27,800
at this address, I would have to travel to that location and pull these cards out and

341
00:24:27,801 --> 00:24:29,080
try to search that information.

342
00:24:29,081 --> 00:24:38,540
One of the great challenges of police work has always been that we accumulated far more information than we were ever able to successfully mine ourselves.

343
00:24:39,240 --> 00:24:48,600
That trapped in the file drawers and the cases of police record rooms were volumes of information that no one could access without manually just digging through them.

344
00:24:49,560 --> 00:24:56,000
By the late 1980s, computers were finally being harnessed to provide rapid access to this wealth of data.

345
00:24:56,001 --> 00:25:01,420
we were able to start using databases and linking those databases and start doing some analysis.

346
00:25:02,360 --> 00:25:06,600
Computers could not only keep track of individuals, evidence, and other criminal records,

347
00:25:07,040 --> 00:25:09,200
they could also map trends and patterns.

348
00:25:09,980 --> 00:25:13,220
For a long time, the police departments, in order to analyze their data,

349
00:25:13,280 --> 00:25:15,080
they'd been sticking pins in a map on a wall.

350
00:25:15,440 --> 00:25:19,040
And now to be able to take that same data, put it on a computer,

351
00:25:19,620 --> 00:25:21,580
graphically present it and analyze it,

352
00:25:21,581 --> 00:25:26,240
and that brings a whole new level of sophistication to crime mapping and crime analysis.

353
00:25:26,840 --> 00:25:29,820
We can look for patterns of activity based on MOs,

354
00:25:30,040 --> 00:25:35,260
and we can do that with data as opposed to with the memory of a particular criminal investigator.

355
00:25:36,120 --> 00:25:39,680
The largest attempt to integrate information on a national level

356
00:25:39,681 --> 00:25:43,720
is the NCIC, the National Crime Information Center.

357
00:25:44,300 --> 00:25:48,840
In the 70s, you still had police departments isolated from each other,

358
00:25:48,841 --> 00:25:53,640
and they each began to develop their own methods for tracking police records.

359
00:25:54,360 --> 00:25:58,760
We have approximately 17,000-plus police forces in the continental United States alone.

360
00:25:59,200 --> 00:26:04,120
It wasn't until the 80s that they really began to look at ways to combine those together,

361
00:26:04,180 --> 00:26:05,240
and it was driven by the feds.

362
00:26:05,720 --> 00:26:08,600
Federal agencies realized that there was a need to try to bind those together.

363
00:26:09,300 --> 00:26:11,980
NCIC was the first attempt to do that, was to combine records.

364
00:26:12,220 --> 00:26:18,280
It's essentially a central repository of information about criminals throughout the U.S.

365
00:26:18,281 --> 00:26:21,900
They have done their best to integrate as many state-level databases, criminal records,

366
00:26:22,380 --> 00:26:24,240
into a national computer infrastructure.

367
00:26:25,060 --> 00:26:26,440
It contains fingerprints.

368
00:26:27,380 --> 00:26:30,000
It contains information on missing persons.

369
00:26:30,600 --> 00:26:33,640
It contains information on bodies that have not been identified.

370
00:26:34,400 --> 00:26:38,460
It contains information on types of crimes and the MO that has occurred.

371
00:26:39,280 --> 00:26:42,620
For the first time, police departments throughout the country

372
00:26:42,621 --> 00:26:46,160
will have the ability to share and access the same data.

373
00:26:46,900 --> 00:26:50,900
But remarkable as they are, the one thing computers have not been able to do

374
00:26:50,901 --> 00:26:54,240
is mend a growing rift between the police and the public.

375
00:26:54,940 --> 00:26:58,020
Coming up, police weapons meet public perception.

376
00:26:59,340 --> 00:27:03,100
Today, the FBI lab investigates over 20,000 cases annually.

377
00:27:04,060 --> 00:27:07,080
Police technology will return on Modern Marvels.

378
00:27:08,200 --> 00:27:12,460
We now return to police technology on Modern Marvels.

379
00:27:12,461 --> 00:27:19,660
One technology that has had an immense impact on how police do their job is television.

380
00:27:19,661 --> 00:27:26,900
In the 1960s, public outcry over images of police using what seemed to be excessive force

381
00:27:26,901 --> 00:27:28,800
led to demands for alternatives.

382
00:27:29,720 --> 00:27:31,000
These are citizens. They have rights.

383
00:27:31,320 --> 00:27:33,400
How do we avoid hurting them, and what can we use to do that?

384
00:27:33,500 --> 00:27:39,480
And so this has really kind of pushed us into trying to explore alternative ways to subdue people

385
00:27:39,481 --> 00:27:44,120
that will minimize the risk to the officer and minimize the damage to the suspect.

386
00:27:44,340 --> 00:27:47,520
Public perception is a driving force in law enforcement.

387
00:27:48,240 --> 00:27:53,220
We are servants of the community, and as soon as we lose perspective of that, we've already failed.

388
00:27:53,940 --> 00:27:57,520
This problem has led to a long and often frustrating search

389
00:27:57,521 --> 00:28:00,980
for what have come to be called less-than-lethal weapons.

390
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:06,580
By the mid-1970s, law enforcement was largely limited to very few technologies,

391
00:28:06,581 --> 00:28:10,620
and that was basically the baton, the slapper, and tear gas.

392
00:28:11,260 --> 00:28:13,920
New, less than lethal weapons, even effective ones,

393
00:28:14,220 --> 00:28:16,540
had to stand the critical test of public opinion.

394
00:28:17,480 --> 00:28:18,740
I'll just give you one example.

395
00:28:19,220 --> 00:28:23,840
Back in the 1970s, there was a device marketed called the electric baton.

396
00:28:24,840 --> 00:28:28,540
It was probably one of the more effective devices that we'd ever had,

397
00:28:28,640 --> 00:28:30,240
but it got nicknamed the cattle prod.

398
00:28:30,840 --> 00:28:32,260
We never recovered from that.

399
00:28:32,261 --> 00:28:42,720
To this day, 30 years later, there's no place in the United States that I'm aware of that's even considering using that because the public perception is so against it.

400
00:28:43,100 --> 00:28:46,040
Beginning about the 1970s, a few other things came out.

401
00:28:46,160 --> 00:28:55,720
There were things like the taser, which is a device that fires to, you can think of them as tiny fish hooks that embed themselves in your skin.

402
00:28:55,860 --> 00:28:58,200
You then fire an electrical charge. It can take you down.

403
00:28:58,360 --> 00:29:01,180
I've been shot with a taser. I can tell you exactly what it feels like.

404
00:29:01,181 --> 00:29:03,180
It's not pleasant.

405
00:29:03,181 --> 00:29:06,180
You're going to get hit with about 3 watts of electricity,

406
00:29:06,181 --> 00:29:08,180
about 50,000 volts.

407
00:29:08,181 --> 00:29:12,180
To me, it felt just like somebody taking his hand and...

408
00:29:12,181 --> 00:29:17,180
just one after the other between 8 and 15 times a second,

409
00:29:17,181 --> 00:29:20,180
and your body just starts quivering.

410
00:29:20,181 --> 00:29:25,180
The problem with things like the Taser are that the range is limited.

411
00:29:25,181 --> 00:29:29,180
The manufacturer may say 15 feet, but it's 10 to 15 feet.

412
00:29:29,181 --> 00:29:30,420
to 15 feet.

413
00:29:30,421 --> 00:29:32,360
This only works 85% of the time, which

414
00:29:32,361 --> 00:29:36,420
means that 15 times out of 100, that's not going to work.

415
00:29:36,421 --> 00:29:39,760
In the 1980s, the quest for the ideal less than lethal weapon

416
00:29:39,761 --> 00:29:42,420
was taken up by researchers at the National Institute

417
00:29:42,421 --> 00:29:44,220
of Justice.

418
00:29:44,221 --> 00:29:47,900
In 1986, the attorney general then convened a less than lethal

419
00:29:47,901 --> 00:29:49,140
conference.

420
00:29:49,141 --> 00:29:51,660
And out of it came a proposal that what we really need

421
00:29:51,661 --> 00:29:55,140
is an anesthetic agent, like an animal dart.

422
00:29:55,141 --> 00:29:56,400
You know how you shoot them at animals

423
00:29:56,401 --> 00:29:58,840
and it puts the animal to sleep and you can carry the animal

424
00:29:58,841 --> 00:29:59,541
away.

425
00:29:59,542 --> 00:30:03,740
What was omitted in those discussions and is omitted in the movies when you see the animal

426
00:30:03,741 --> 00:30:09,252
euthanized or anesthetized and moved is that half of them die in the process.

427
00:30:09,253 --> 00:30:10,540
Another idea was a

428
00:30:10,541 --> 00:30:13,360
human glue gun that would shoot sticky foam.

429
00:30:13,361 --> 00:30:16,500
We developed a sticky foam device that can launch

430
00:30:16,501 --> 00:30:22,020
sticky foam for a distance of 35 to 60 feet and it really does stick the criminal in place and the

431
00:30:22,021 --> 00:30:25,898
more he struggles with it the more it sticks him in place but it's got some problems.

432
00:30:25,899 --> 00:30:26,599
One of them

433
00:30:26,600 --> 00:30:28,640
is it's very hard to clean up.

434
00:30:28,641 --> 00:30:31,400
It takes about 10 minutes per square inch.

435
00:30:31,401 --> 00:30:33,800
So then the next question is, how long does it stay sticky?

436
00:30:33,801 --> 00:30:36,320
How long do we have to wait before it's unsticky?

437
00:30:36,321 --> 00:30:38,400
And the answer is a very long time.

438
00:30:38,401 --> 00:30:40,620
Scientific terms, eternity.

439
00:30:40,621 --> 00:30:42,280
So we've established unequivocally

440
00:30:42,281 --> 00:30:45,640
that this thing that came up in every report works,

441
00:30:45,641 --> 00:30:47,580
but it's not practical.

442
00:30:47,581 --> 00:30:49,500
For a long time, law enforcement,

443
00:30:49,501 --> 00:30:51,460
beginning as far back in the 60s,

444
00:30:51,461 --> 00:30:54,520
began to introduce blunt trauma weapons.

445
00:30:54,521 --> 00:30:57,860
is a little bean bags or rubber bullets that you'd shoot.

446
00:30:57,861 --> 00:31:00,400
The notion being you strike them with a rubber bullet,

447
00:31:00,401 --> 00:31:02,620
that reduces the likelihood of a fatality.

448
00:31:03,700 --> 00:31:06,160
But because their trajectories quickly decay,

449
00:31:06,161 --> 00:31:09,340
bean bag rounds only work at very limited ranges.

450
00:31:10,740 --> 00:31:13,040
The more sophisticated blunt trauma weapon

451
00:31:13,041 --> 00:31:15,480
is the ring airfoil projectile.

452
00:31:15,481 --> 00:31:16,840
It's basically an airplane wing

453
00:31:16,841 --> 00:31:18,980
that's curled into a donut shape.

454
00:31:18,981 --> 00:31:20,840
And then he fires it off a launcher

455
00:31:20,841 --> 00:31:23,540
that puts a 5,000 RPM spin on it

456
00:31:23,541 --> 00:31:26,140
that actually flies to the target.

457
00:31:26,141 --> 00:31:28,600
This means it can be used at much greater distances

458
00:31:28,601 --> 00:31:33,140
to safely but effectively knock the wind out of somebody.

459
00:31:33,141 --> 00:31:36,740
In the late 1970s, police began using pepper spray,

460
00:31:36,741 --> 00:31:40,640
a substance found naturally in cayenne peppers.

461
00:31:40,641 --> 00:31:43,460
The first federal agency that ever employed it

462
00:31:43,461 --> 00:31:45,920
was the US Post Office.

463
00:31:45,921 --> 00:31:49,420
And they provided them to mailmen.

464
00:31:49,421 --> 00:31:51,820
In fact, while I was in college, I was a mailman briefly,

465
00:31:51,821 --> 00:31:53,360
and I actually had some of that.

466
00:31:53,361 --> 00:31:56,220
and they used it to keep dogs at bay.

467
00:31:56,221 --> 00:31:58,760
It was used initially to control dogs.

468
00:31:58,761 --> 00:32:02,040
It causes temporary discomfort

469
00:32:02,041 --> 00:32:04,400
and loss of the ability of the dog

470
00:32:04,401 --> 00:32:06,180
to tell where the postman is.

471
00:32:06,181 --> 00:32:09,040
So someone, and I don't even know who it was

472
00:32:09,041 --> 00:32:10,500
that first came out with it, said,

473
00:32:10,501 --> 00:32:13,100
gee, maybe this could be something that a cop could use.

474
00:32:13,101 --> 00:32:18,120
The first time I ever saw it used was in the 1980s,

475
00:32:18,121 --> 00:32:19,440
and it was unbelievably effective.

476
00:32:19,441 --> 00:32:21,680
It was virtually instantaneous.

477
00:32:21,681 --> 00:32:24,560
pepper spray now comes packed in pepper balls

478
00:32:24,561 --> 00:32:27,660
that can be fired using a standard paintball gun.

479
00:32:27,661 --> 00:32:29,760
It gives us the ability of striking a target

480
00:32:29,761 --> 00:32:32,220
at greater than the 15 feet that the spray does.

481
00:32:32,221 --> 00:32:34,960
It strikes hard enough to cause a little bit of pain,

482
00:32:34,961 --> 00:32:36,360
not enough to cause compliance,

483
00:32:36,361 --> 00:32:38,120
but enough to get your attention.

484
00:32:38,121 --> 00:32:40,840
And it hurts and you involuntarily gasp.

485
00:32:40,841 --> 00:32:43,060
At the same time it bursts, it releases this cloud

486
00:32:43,061 --> 00:32:47,680
of just finely ground pepper dust and one good whiff.

487
00:32:47,681 --> 00:32:51,080
And that's all that's in your mind from that point on.

488
00:32:51,680 --> 00:32:54,840
Another device shoots a stream of pepper spray mixed with water.

489
00:32:55,600 --> 00:32:59,840
Called the option, it attaches to the barrel of a standard service revolver,

490
00:33:00,440 --> 00:33:03,000
giving an officer both the security of a lethal weapon

491
00:33:03,001 --> 00:33:06,800
and also the alternative of using less than lethal force.

492
00:33:07,860 --> 00:33:10,760
Put the gas down! Put it down!

493
00:33:12,440 --> 00:33:14,900
Another less than lethal weapon still in development

494
00:33:14,901 --> 00:33:17,680
is a noxious chemical called dragon's breath.

495
00:33:18,400 --> 00:33:19,540
This stuff will gag a maggot.

496
00:33:19,541 --> 00:33:31,193
I mean, I try to think of some visual way or verbal way of describing it, but words fail me, because unless you smell this stuff at least once, there's no way I can

497
00:33:31,194 --> 00:33:33,680
convey to you how bad stink can be.

498
00:33:34,620 --> 00:33:38,200
One potential use is to stop an agitator from inciting a mob.

499
00:33:38,880 --> 00:33:44,860
We don't have to arrest this individual, because when we make him stink so bad that he can't influence the crowd, he's a non-entity.

500
00:33:44,861 --> 00:33:46,200
He's no longer a factor.

501
00:33:46,600 --> 00:33:48,520
And he doesn't have to go to jail.

502
00:33:48,521 --> 00:33:52,700
he's probably going to go home and wash because he can't stand to be around himself.

503
00:33:54,520 --> 00:33:58,880
Another problem that technology is struggling to solve is the high-speed chase.

504
00:33:59,800 --> 00:34:05,720
To avoid civilian casualties, many departments have greatly restricted officers from engaging in these pursuits.

505
00:34:06,260 --> 00:34:08,780
But a better strategy has been hard to come by.

506
00:34:10,360 --> 00:34:16,780
In early days of automobiles, police tried out a device designed to hook on to a fleeing vehicle and drag it to a halt.

507
00:34:18,520 --> 00:34:21,520
It never caught on.

508
00:34:21,521 --> 00:34:26,520
A more recent invention is the road spike, a portable strip that can be rolled across

509
00:34:26,521 --> 00:34:28,520
a road and remotely triggered.

510
00:34:28,521 --> 00:34:32,520
Then you wait for the subject to get there, then pop it up, take out the subject's tires,

511
00:34:32,521 --> 00:34:35,520
and then put it down before you take out police officer tires.

512
00:34:35,521 --> 00:34:39,520
It would deflate their tires, hopefully without causing a blowout.

513
00:34:39,521 --> 00:34:44,520
The car would then be forced to go a bit slower, and so then you could stop the car.

514
00:34:44,521 --> 00:34:48,040
That improves somewhat over years by putting in hollow spikes,

515
00:34:48,280 --> 00:34:50,620
which allow for controlled deflation of the tires

516
00:34:50,621 --> 00:34:53,620
and dramatically reduce the likelihood of a blowout or loss of control.

517
00:34:53,940 --> 00:34:55,940
The biggest problem is you have to be there first.

518
00:34:56,200 --> 00:34:59,360
And if we knew where the guy was going, why would we chase him to begin with?

519
00:34:59,500 --> 00:35:04,960
And we're going to test every technology that people think can stop a car

520
00:35:04,961 --> 00:35:08,000
so we can determine which really work and which are really safe.

521
00:35:08,001 --> 00:35:10,420
And then we'll let police make those determinations.

522
00:35:11,140 --> 00:35:13,680
In the meantime, we're still looking to try to develop things

523
00:35:13,681 --> 00:35:17,780
that would allow you to point something at the car and zap it and have it stop.

524
00:35:18,120 --> 00:35:21,300
We're nowhere near having that yet, but that's where we'd like to be.

525
00:35:21,840 --> 00:35:25,320
Another area of research involves harnessing the power of light.

526
00:35:26,180 --> 00:35:31,040
The laser dazzler uses a green flashing beam to temporarily blind and disorient.

527
00:35:31,660 --> 00:35:35,240
But the ideal less-than-lethal weapon is still a distant dream.

528
00:35:35,560 --> 00:35:39,940
I think in the future we're going to see some sort of short-range device

529
00:35:39,941 --> 00:35:42,800
that will do the same basic things that a taser will do,

530
00:35:42,801 --> 00:35:46,160
or the pepper spray will do that they can't do right now.

531
00:35:46,600 --> 00:35:53,060
The standard for the perfect non-lethal device is probably the phaser.

532
00:35:53,780 --> 00:35:57,760
The phaser from the Star Trek TV series used a beam of energy

533
00:35:57,761 --> 00:36:01,400
to totally disable its victim without causing any bodily harm.

534
00:36:02,180 --> 00:36:06,540
That's the holy grail or the unholy grail, depending on your point of view.

535
00:36:06,541 --> 00:36:12,500
And if you lined up 100 cops, probably 99 would say, that's what I really want.

536
00:36:12,501 --> 00:36:14,520
And that is a very, very, very hard thing.

537
00:36:14,600 --> 00:36:19,740
But at the same time, I'm a scientist and I have faith in the future.

538
00:36:19,741 --> 00:36:24,380
And if you look at some of the things that we take for common and granted for today,

539
00:36:24,920 --> 00:36:28,800
you know, five years ago, ten years ago, you say, nah, you never do that.

540
00:36:29,660 --> 00:36:32,660
Coming up, a look at the police car of the future.

541
00:36:34,680 --> 00:36:38,740
We now return to police technology on Modern Marvels.

542
00:36:38,741 --> 00:36:44,140
In the last few decades, the police car has become a launching pad for new technologies.

543
00:36:45,140 --> 00:36:55,360
It has evolved from being a means of transportation to being a means of rapid response to now being an information center.

544
00:36:58,300 --> 00:37:07,360
In the 1960s and 70s, police cars went from being off-the-lot models to customized police packages with improved engine power and durability.

545
00:37:08,220 --> 00:37:16,160
They featured sophisticated multiband radios that allowed two-way communication not only with dispatch, but also with other units in the field.

546
00:37:17,800 --> 00:37:23,440
The early 90s saw the addition of a controversial new device, in-car video cameras.

547
00:37:24,060 --> 00:37:28,380
Police officers don't like the idea that someone is going to constantly monitor their every activity.

548
00:37:28,620 --> 00:37:33,040
Five years ago, six years ago, the thought that when I get out of a vehicle and I go to the window and say,

549
00:37:33,041 --> 00:37:36,900
sir, can you please hand me your ID, and that camera looking right at me,

550
00:37:37,700 --> 00:37:38,920
officers weren't very happy with that.

551
00:37:39,040 --> 00:37:40,200
But now they've seen the benefits.

552
00:37:40,280 --> 00:37:41,460
I think most of them are very happy with it.

553
00:37:42,300 --> 00:37:45,480
In several instances, images captured by in-car video

554
00:37:45,481 --> 00:37:47,920
have provided the critical evidence in police shootings.

555
00:37:57,000 --> 00:37:58,440
Video has just been shot!

556
00:37:59,980 --> 00:38:02,800
Another innovation was an in-car computer terminal.

557
00:38:03,040 --> 00:38:08,760
that allowed officers in the field to be directly linked to local, state, even national databases.

558
00:38:09,320 --> 00:38:14,100
The NOSER can run a tag number on a car and get the registration information on that vehicle,

559
00:38:14,320 --> 00:38:16,540
which would include the owner of the vehicle.

560
00:38:16,980 --> 00:38:21,440
You then can search the database of the owner of the vehicle to see their driving record,

561
00:38:21,580 --> 00:38:23,540
whether or not their license is valid at that time.

562
00:38:23,541 --> 00:38:27,780
Has this person been stopped before and released with a warning?

563
00:38:27,781 --> 00:38:33,520
Does this person have any outstanding parking violations on the car and that type of information?

564
00:38:34,480 --> 00:38:38,700
This abundance of technology, valuable as it was, created a new problem.

565
00:38:39,220 --> 00:38:42,220
The cockpit of the car began to get more and more crowded.

566
00:38:43,140 --> 00:38:44,720
And you had this array of switches.

567
00:38:45,240 --> 00:38:49,040
You had switches for the siren, switches for the light, and you had the radios and radio controls.

568
00:38:49,400 --> 00:38:50,780
Each one was a separate box.

569
00:38:51,120 --> 00:38:52,400
It was incredibly confusing.

570
00:38:53,320 --> 00:38:57,480
There are some people who believe some officers have been killed in traffic trying to control all of this stuff

571
00:38:57,481 --> 00:38:58,520
because it was so hard to do.

572
00:38:58,840 --> 00:39:01,880
To address this problem, the Texas Transportation Institute

573
00:39:01,881 --> 00:39:07,200
began a project for developing a simpler and safer police car called Alert.

574
00:39:08,120 --> 00:39:13,360
We were looking at ways to bring additional technologies into the police vehicle.

575
00:39:13,820 --> 00:39:17,240
The problem we ran into was that there wasn't enough room inside the car

576
00:39:17,241 --> 00:39:18,680
for the equipment that was already there,

577
00:39:18,780 --> 00:39:20,820
and we were talking about putting additional equipment in.

578
00:39:21,180 --> 00:39:24,940
So we felt the first thing we had to do was address the space problem inside the vehicle.

579
00:39:24,941 --> 00:39:31,520
We've moved out, if you will, all the traditional equipment, the overhead light control, the radio, the siren control, the radar.

580
00:39:31,880 --> 00:39:33,760
All of that equipment was moved to the trunk.

581
00:39:34,260 --> 00:39:37,015
The interface for the officer is actually a computer screen.

582
00:39:37,016 --> 00:39:38,300
It's a touchscreen computer.

583
00:39:38,660 --> 00:39:43,248
Now I have one screen, and the screen controls the lights. It controls the siren.

584
00:39:43,249 --> 00:39:46,760
It controls my radio frequencies. It controls my video camera.

585
00:39:47,140 --> 00:39:50,880
He sees a violator, somebody exceeding the speed limit.

586
00:39:50,881 --> 00:39:58,160
Instead of using the radar controls and displays, he's using the alert system to control the radar functions and also the display.

587
00:39:58,520 --> 00:40:00,300
Everything would be controlled off that computer.

588
00:40:00,980 --> 00:40:04,140
He now goes into a high-speed pursuit. He tells him I'm going into a pursuit.

589
00:40:04,300 --> 00:40:08,080
The computer decides what the right setting is for the lights and sets them up.

590
00:40:09,940 --> 00:40:17,580
Once he gets the violator stopped, the system could then transmit via GPS the information on his location back to the dispatch area

591
00:40:17,581 --> 00:40:20,500
and have a visual display of the location of the officer.

592
00:40:20,880 --> 00:40:23,120
and all of that done without any input from the officer.

593
00:40:23,760 --> 00:40:26,440
When you exit the car, the cameras will come on for you.

594
00:40:26,540 --> 00:40:27,900
It goes in auto record mode.

595
00:40:29,040 --> 00:40:33,480
Alert also allows an officer to access this technology when he is away from the car.

596
00:40:33,860 --> 00:40:38,100
What we've done is we've taken the capabilities of the officer inside the car

597
00:40:38,101 --> 00:40:40,140
and moved it outside of the car with a handheld computer.

598
00:40:40,141 --> 00:40:44,920
You can then enter information directly from a driver's license into the system.

599
00:40:45,040 --> 00:40:47,340
There's no manual entry necessary by the officer.

600
00:40:47,480 --> 00:40:50,340
This cuts down on the time the officer is on the stop

601
00:40:50,341 --> 00:40:53,560
and it also increases the accuracy of the information being captured.

602
00:40:53,920 --> 00:40:57,320
There are some areas that are getting into automatic pay.

603
00:40:57,740 --> 00:41:02,920
When an officer makes a traffic stop, he can get a credit card from the individual,

604
00:41:03,360 --> 00:41:09,040
and it will actually deduct the amount of the fine automatically from the person's account.

605
00:41:09,640 --> 00:41:13,740
The alert system is also able to capture and transmit digital imagery.

606
00:41:14,620 --> 00:41:17,720
Currently, if I'm dispatched to a missing child,

607
00:41:17,721 --> 00:41:20,940
I go to, say, to the residents of the parents.

608
00:41:21,420 --> 00:41:22,780
The mother tells me your child's missing.

609
00:41:22,920 --> 00:41:24,820
The first thing I'm going to ask for is a photograph of the child.

610
00:41:26,200 --> 00:41:30,700
At this time, I don't have the capability of distributing that photograph other than manually.

611
00:41:30,800 --> 00:41:34,960
I have to go get copies made and distribute those copies to officers in the field,

612
00:41:35,040 --> 00:41:36,020
and that's very time-consuming.

613
00:41:36,480 --> 00:41:39,360
With this type of system, what we could do is go to the car,

614
00:41:40,140 --> 00:41:42,140
capture the image digitally in the system,

615
00:41:42,360 --> 00:41:45,140
and then transmit that image directly to the officers in the field.

616
00:41:45,141 --> 00:41:47,980
And we can do that in a matter of minutes rather than in a matter of hours.

617
00:41:48,980 --> 00:41:53,140
In the future, such technology could lead to new ways of identifying suspects.

618
00:41:53,900 --> 00:42:01,280
Today, an officer, when he or she stops someone, we base our information on what that person tells us.

619
00:42:01,580 --> 00:42:04,420
And also through any identification they carry.

620
00:42:04,940 --> 00:42:10,160
And you're really relying on your ability to know whether or not that person is telling you the truth.

621
00:42:10,260 --> 00:42:12,160
And sometimes that's difficult to do.

622
00:42:12,161 --> 00:42:20,620
The Arlington County, Virginia Sheriff's Department now has a computerized mugshot system capable of identifying someone using only their picture.

623
00:42:21,200 --> 00:42:27,460
After the picture is captured, we'll plot a number of points on the face, the facial features.

624
00:42:28,020 --> 00:42:37,240
It will take those plotted points and search through the database to see if that person's picture has ever been taken before and if it's in our database.

625
00:42:37,241 --> 00:42:43,240
You've got a new scar on your face, you have a mustache, you change your hairstyle.

626
00:42:43,241 --> 00:42:47,240
It doesn't plot that, it plots the facial structure.

627
00:42:47,241 --> 00:42:51,240
Another portable device allows fingerprints to be captured in the field.

628
00:42:51,241 --> 00:42:55,240
If we were able to collect their fingerprint at the scene, and we run that through the database,

629
00:42:55,241 --> 00:43:00,240
and if that person's fingerprint is in the database, then we know who we have.

630
00:43:00,241 --> 00:43:06,240
Other new technologies are allowing police to see what until now has been hidden.

631
00:43:06,241 --> 00:43:13,620
Forward-looking infrared radar, or FLIR, uses heat energy to make bodies visible, even in the dark.

632
00:43:14,260 --> 00:43:19,060
Their advantage, of course, is that you can see people who might be hidden behind a bush

633
00:43:19,061 --> 00:43:23,320
because the heat will show up or maybe they hide in the brush somewhere.

634
00:43:23,400 --> 00:43:24,680
They'll show up because they're a hot spot.

635
00:43:25,020 --> 00:43:29,600
On the horizon are both special magnetic and acoustic technologies,

636
00:43:29,601 --> 00:43:33,600
along with infrared technologies, that can look for a weapon on your body.

637
00:43:33,601 --> 00:43:39,900
Another upcoming technology is a sensor mounted inside a van that can smell faint

638
00:43:39,901 --> 00:43:44,354
traces of illegal drugs like crack and PCP.

639
00:43:44,355 --> 00:43:48,600
But ironically, despite this abundance of

640
00:43:48,601 --> 00:43:53,100
technology, police work in many places is returning to the days of the beat cop.

641
00:43:53,101 --> 00:43:58,680
The last decade has seen a resurgence of community policing, putting police back in

642
00:43:58,681 --> 00:44:00,680
touch with the neighborhoods they serve.

643
00:44:00,681 --> 00:44:06,840
The police department can't control crime and lower the rate of crime on its own.

644
00:44:06,841 --> 00:44:10,360
It needs the help of the community and that's what we're there to do to be a partnership

645
00:44:10,361 --> 00:44:11,360
with the community.

646
00:44:11,361 --> 00:44:16,620
At the end of the day there's nothing more important than our officers ability to relate

647
00:44:16,621 --> 00:44:19,900
with a diverse human constituency.

648
00:44:19,901 --> 00:44:25,920
That their authority is based as much on their ability to be accepted as a credible person

649
00:44:25,921 --> 00:44:28,020
as it is on the fact that they are wearing a badge

650
00:44:28,021 --> 00:44:29,620
and carrying a weapon.

651
00:44:29,621 --> 00:44:30,620
I'm here to help you, okay?

652
00:44:30,621 --> 00:44:33,160
I'm not here to beat you up or anything, man.

653
00:44:33,161 --> 00:44:34,420
For all its advances,

654
00:44:34,421 --> 00:44:37,460
technology at the dawn of the 21st century

655
00:44:37,461 --> 00:44:40,040
has only accentuated the critical importance

656
00:44:40,041 --> 00:44:43,300
of the human being who wears the uniform.

